---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Source from '../components/Source.astro';
import routing from '../../snippets/routing.ts?raw';
import server from '../../snippets/server.ts?raw';
import checkout from '../../snippets/checkout.tsx?raw';
import typeErrors from '../../snippets/type-errors.ts?raw';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const repository = 'https://github.com/farisaziz12/tenderlane';
const example = `${repository}/tree/main/examples/react-checkout-multi-psp`;

// The part of type-errors.ts that carries the errors; the imports add nothing here.
const typeErrorsBody = typeErrors.slice(typeErrors.indexOf('createRulesRouter({'));
---

<Layout title="Tenderlane: route a checkout between payment providers from app state">
  <div class="container home">
    <header class="intro">
      <p class="status">
        Alpha proof of concept. Not published to npm, not production-ready.
        <a href={`${base}/docs/limitations`}>What is missing</a>
      </p>
      <h1>
        Tenderlane selects the payment provider and checkout flow from your application&rsquo;s
        state.
      </h1>
      <p class="lede">
        It is a headless TypeScript library. You write routing rules as data. A client state
        machine runs whichever provider the rules select behind one <code>submit()</code>, and a
        server handler re-checks the route and sets the price, so the browser never sends an
        amount. Stripe and Polar adapters and React bindings exist today.
      </p>
      <p class="links">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={`${base}/docs/getting-started`}>Run the example</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={`${base}/docs/architecture`}>Architecture</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={repository}>Source</a>
      </p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p>
        A checkout that uses two providers, or two flows of one provider, grows the same
        conditional in three places: the component that decides what to render, the submit
        handler, and the API route that creates the session. Each provider SDK has its own
        session object and its own meaning of &ldquo;done&rdquo;, and nothing checks that the
        three places agree.
      </p>
      <p>
        Tenderlane moves that decision into one router that the browser and the server both
        evaluate, and puts every provider behind the same two interfaces.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What you write</h2>

      <h3>1. Rules: which provider and flow, for which state</h3>
      <p>
        Plain data, first match wins. <code>hosted</code> redirects to the provider&rsquo;s page;
        <code>inline</code> mounts the provider&rsquo;s payment element in yours.
      </p>
      <Source code={routing} file="docs-site/snippets/routing.ts" />

      <h3>2. A server endpoint: the trust boundary</h3>
      <p>
        The browser sends <code>{'{ sku, quantity }'}</code>. The handler prices it from your
        catalog, evaluates the same rules, and only then calls the provider with your secret key.
      </p>
      <Source code={server} file="docs-site/snippets/server.ts" />

      <h3>3. One form for every route</h3>
      <p>
        The form renders no markup of its own. When app state changes the route, the same
        component switches between a redirect and an inline payment element.
      </p>
      <Source code={checkout} file="docs-site/snippets/checkout.tsx" />
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What the compiler checks</h2>
      <p>
        Provider ids are inferred as literals from the providers you register, and the router is
        checked against them. These are real errors: the file compiles only because each one is
        expected.
      </p>
      <Source code={typeErrorsBody} file="docs-site/snippets/type-errors.ts" />
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Where it sits</h2>
      <pre class="flow" aria-label="Data flow">{`browser                                         server
───────                                         ──────
app state { country, ... }
   │
router ─► route { provider, flow }
   │
client state machine ── submit() ──────────────► handler.POST
   │                    { provider,              1. drop any client amount, validate
   │                      items: [{ sku, qty }] } 2. derive the server's context
   │                                             3. same router: is this the right provider?
   │                                             4. price items from the catalog
   │                                             5. server adapter ─► PSP API (secret key)
   ◄─────────── { id, status, url | clientSecret } ◄┘
   │
hosted: redirect to url      inline: confirm in the provider's element`}</pre>
      <p>
        Tenderlane is a library in your app and your API route. It is not a service, does not
        proxy payments, and never sees card data: that stays in the provider&rsquo;s page or
        iframe.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="columns">
      <div>
        <h2>Works today</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Rules router shared by browser and server</li>
          <li>Headless client state machine, typed as a discriminated union</li>
          <li>React provider, hook and form</li>
          <li>Stripe: Checkout (hosted) and Payment Element (inline)</li>
          <li>Polar: hosted checkout, one product per checkout</li>
          <li>Server-side pricing from a catalog you define, or from Stripe or Polar</li>
          <li>Server-side confirmation on return: an order hook and a status lookup</li>
          <li>An adapter API for other providers</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>Does not exist yet</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Webhooks: a server-side status lookup exists, push notification does not</li>
          <li>Subscriptions, refunds, saved payment methods</li>
          <li>Failover between providers</li>
          <li>Any provider other than Stripe and Polar</li>
          <li>npm releases of this API</li>
          <li>A test against a live PSP sandbox in CI (the PSP SDKs are mocked)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Try it</h2>
      <p>
        The example runs without any account, against a built-in demo PSP that is labelled as
        such and moves no money. Stripe test keys switch on the real Stripe routes.
      </p>
      <pre class="flow">{`git clone https://github.com/farisaziz12/tenderlane.git
cd tenderlane
pnpm install
pnpm example        # http://localhost:3000`}</pre>
      <p>
        <a href={`${base}/docs/getting-started`}>Getting started</a> walks through what you see.
        The <a href={example}>example source</a> is under 500 lines.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .home {
    max-width: 820px;
    padding-top: 56px;
    color: var(--text-1);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .intro {
    margin-bottom: 56px;
  }
  .status {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px 10px;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-2);
    margin: 0 0 20px;
  }
  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(26px, 4.2vw, 38px);
    line-height: 1.18;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--text-0);
    margin: 0 0 18px;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 17px;
    margin: 0 0 24px;
  }
  .links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 10px;
    margin: 0;
  }
  section {
    margin: 0 0 56px;
  }
  h2 {
    font-size: 22px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text-0);
    margin: 0 0 12px;
  }
  h3 {
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text-0);
    margin: 32px 0 6px;
  }
  p {
    margin: 0 0 12px;
  }
  a:not(.btn) {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
  code {
    font: 0.88em var(--font-mono);
    color: var(--text-0);
    background: var(--bg-2);
    padding: 1px 5px;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
  ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 20px;
  }
  li {
    margin: 6px 0;
  }
  .columns {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr));
    gap: 32px;
  }
  .flow {
    margin: 16px 0;
    padding: 16px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line-2);
    border-radius: 8px;
    background: var(--code-bg);
    color: var(--text-1);
    font: 12.5px/1.6 var(--font-mono);
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
</style>
